"Linearization Explains Fine-Tuning in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08239v1)\nciteturn31649view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view2\",\"lineno\":135}); Total lines: 810\nL109: ###### Proof.\nL110: \nL111: See Appendix cite18†B . ∎\nL112: \nL113: In fine-tuning, the objective in (cite51†1 ) is non-convex in the parameters. However, according to Theorem cite52†1 , a sufficient condition for regularized fine-tuning to have non-increasing $\\|f_{\\theta_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|^{2}_{2}$, is that at step $t$, we have $\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta}_{t})\\left(\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right)\\geq 0$ or $\\lambda=0$. Therefore, we modify the regularization step in (cite56†5 ) as\nL114:  | $$\\bm{\\theta}_{t}=\\begin{cases}\\widetilde{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}-\\lambda\\,\\eta\\left(\\widetilde{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right)&\\text{if }\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta}_{t})\\left(\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right)\\geq 0,\\\\\nL115: \\widetilde{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}&\\text{if }\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta}_{t})\\left(\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right)<0\\end{cases}.$$  |  | (8)\nL116: This is a selective regularization scheme, which decides on regularization of each trainable parameter based on the direction of its gradient. Intuitively, the inner product $\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}\\widetilde{\\mathcal{R}}(\\bm{\\theta}_{t})\\left(\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\right)$ reflects how aligned the current progress is with the negative gradient update direction. A negative inner product signals consistent improvement in loss, while a positive value suggests movement toward a higher-loss region.\nL117: As a result, in order to have non-increasing loss at step $t$, (cite57†8 ) regularizes only the trainable parameters with positive inner product values [cite41†9 ].\nL118: ###### Theorem 2.\nL119: \nL120: Consider the selectively regularized fine-tuning solution, under squared loss. Denote the instantaneous value of the regularization parameter by $\\lambda_{t}$, which can be either $0$ or $\\lambda$. If $f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})$ is Lip$(f)$-Lipschitz in an $\\ell_{2}$-ball of radius $r$ around pretrained parameters $\\bm{\\theta}_{0}$, we have\nL121:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|\\;\\leq\\;2\\,\\text{Lip}(f)\\,\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|\\;\\int_{0}^{t}e^{-(\\Lambda_{t}-\\Lambda_{s})}\\,ds,\\quad\\textrm{where }\\Lambda_{t}=\\int_{0}^{t}\\lambda_{s}ds.$  |  | (9)\nL122: \nL123: In the special case when $\\lambda_{t}=\\lambda$ (remains constant), we obtain\nL124: \nL125:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|\\leq 2\\,\\text{Lip}(f)\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|\\frac{1-e^{-\\lambda t}}{\\lambda}.$  |  | (10)\nL126: ###### Proof.\nL127: \nL128: See Appendix cite19†C . ∎\nL129: \nL130: Intuitively, one can think of (cite58†9 ) as placing the “average” $\\lambda$, $\\frac{1}{t}\\Lambda_{t}$ in (cite59†10 ). In what follows, we adhere to (cite59†10 ), for simplicity. Note that in conventional fine-tuning, $\\lambda=0$. Since $\\lim_{\\lambda\\to 0}\\frac{1-e^{-\\lambda t}}{\\lambda}=t$ the bound in (cite59†10 ) recovers\nL131: \nL132:  | $\\displaystyle\\|\\bm{\\theta}_{t}-\\bm{\\theta}_{0}\\|\\leq 2\\,\\text{Lip}(f)\\,\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|\\,t,$  |  | (11)\nL133: which follows Theorem 2.3 in [cite48†16 ], in the case when the rescaling parameter is one. In other words, regularization and rescaling both manifest the lazy training phenomenon, but regularization maintains much closer proximity, since $\\frac{1-e^{-\\lambda t}}{\\lambda}$ is bounded, unlike $t$.\nL134: Theorem cite53†2 is primarily a building block for Theorem cite54†3 ; it simply says that while the solution may deviate from the origin under regularization, we can bound that deviation. Theorems cite54†3 and cite60†4 show that we can jump from a deviation from the initialization bound, which is the direct objective of regularization, to a bound on how far the NTK solution and the regularized fine-tuning solutions are, i.e., the indirect but main benefit of regularization.\nL135: This is what allows us to place the approximation of Section §cite9†4 on a stronger footing than prior works [cite42†10 , cite43†11 , cite44†12 , cite45†13 ] that assume that such proximity simply holds. For simplicity, we consider the $\\lambda$ constant case to prove Theorems cite54†3 and cite60†4 .\nL136: ###### Theorem 3.\nL137: \nL138: Under the squared loss, if $f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})$ and $\\nabla f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})$ are Lip$(f)$-Lipschitz and Lip$(\\nabla f)$-Lipschitz in an $\\ell_{2}$-ball of radius $r$ around $\\bm{\\theta}_{0}$ respectively, we have\nL139:  | $$\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})\\|\\leq b\\left(t-\\frac{1-e^{-\\lambda t}}{\\lambda}\\,\\right),\\quad\\textrm{where}$$  |  | (12)\nL140:  | $\\displaystyle b=2\\,\\text{Lip}(f)^{2}\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|\\ \\left(\\frac{4}{\\lambda}\\text{Lip}(\\nabla f)\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|+1\\right).$  |  | (13)\nL141: ###### Proof.\nL142: \nL143: See Appendix cite20†D . ∎\nL144: \nL145: In the following theorem, we show that, for a proper choice of the regularization parameter $\\lambda$, linearization of fine-tuning only depends on the local properties of $f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})$ around $\\bm{\\theta}_{0}$.\nL146: ###### Theorem 4.\nL147: \nL148: Let the loss be the squared loss, $f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})$ and $\\nabla f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{X})$ be Lip$(f)$-Lipschitz and Lip$(\\nabla f)$-Lipschitz, respectively, in an $\\ell_{2}$-ball of radius $r$ around $\\bm{\\theta}_{0}$. Define\nL149: \nL150:  | $$\\lambda_{\\circ}=\\frac{2\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\|~\\text{Lip}(f)}{r}.$$  |\nL151: \nL152:   * •\nL153: \nL154: If $\\lambda\\geq\\lambda_{\\circ}$, then for all $t$, the following holds.\nL155: \nL156:   * •\nL157: If $\\lambda<\\lambda_{\\circ}$, then the following holds for $t\\leq\\frac{1}{\\lambda}\\ln\\frac{1}{1-\\lambda/\\lambda_{\\circ}}$\nL158: \nL159:  | $$\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})\\|\\leq 2\\,\\text{Lip}(f)^{2}\\|\\mathbf{Y}(0)-\\mathbf{Y}\\|\\ \\left(\\frac{4}{\\lambda}\\text{Lip}(\\nabla f)\\|\\mathbf{Y}(0)-\\mathbf{Y}\\|+\\,1\\right)t.$$  |\nL160: \nL161: In particular, for $\\lambda\\geq\\lambda_{\\circ}$, the bound from Theorem cite54†3 always holds and simplifies to:\nL162:  | $$\\|f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})-\\bar{f}_{\\bar{\\bm{\\theta}}_{t}}(\\mathbf{X})\\|\\leq 2\\,\\text{Lip}(f)\\widetilde{R}(\\bm{\\theta}_{0})\\left(2r\\text{Lip}(\\nabla f)+\\,\\text{Lip}(f)\\right)t.$$  |\nL163: ###### Proof.\nL164: \nL165: See Appendix cite21†E . ∎\nL166: \nL167: While the distance between the pretrained and fine-tuned models can be substantial, the results illustrate that this gap can be effectively constrained through regularization. Under this condition, fine-tuning can be modeled by NTK regression [cite47†15 ], as discussed in the following section.\nL168: The takeaway of Theorem cite60†4 is that the regularization determines the time scale at which the approximation holds, as well as how tight the approximation is. Theorem cite60†4 , at first glance, seems to suggest that a larger value of $\\lambda$ is better to bring NTK and regularized trajectories together.\nL169: However, a larger $\\lambda$ means that we effectively force ourselves to remain in an even smaller ball around $\\bm{\\theta}_{0}$ and may suffer in terms of fine-tuning performance (see $\\lambda=50$ in Table cite61†1 ). The “sweet spot” regularization parameter can be read from Theorem cite60†4 . It should be proportional to the smoothness of $f$, the pretrained error, and inversely proportional to the radius where the smoothness assumptions hold.\nL170: While $\\lambda_{\\circ}$ is in terms of Lip$(f)$, Theorem cite54†3 and (cite62†13 ) show that there are also diminishing returns for pushing $\\lambda$ beyond Lip$(\\nabla f)$, due to the $+1$ in this term.\nL171: ## 4 Fine-Tuning Meets Neural Tangent Kernel Regression\nL172: \nL173: We formally define the fine-tuning problem as a regularized function estimation in the reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS), $\\mathcal{H}$, generated by the NTK, $\\mathbf{k}(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x}^{\\prime})=\\nabla f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{x})\\nabla f_{\\bm{\\theta}}(\\mathbf{x}^{\\prime})^{\\top}$.\nL174: As shown in the previous section, regularized fine-tuning is in the linearized regime. Therefore, $f_{\\bm{\\theta}^{\\star}}(\\cdot)$ can be approximated by its dual in the tangent space, as shown in Figure cite63†1 . We can interpret the linearized model as a kernel method with a feature map given by $\\phi(\\mathbf{x})=\\nabla_{\\bm{\\theta}}f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}\\left(\\mathbf{x}\\right)$. The corresponding kernel induced by this feature map is the NTK. Jacot et al.\nL175: in  [cite47†15 ], showed that gradient descent with an infinitesimally small learning rate is equivalent to performing kernel regression with the fixed NTK. Thus, the empirical risk minimization (cite51†1 ) is approximated by kernel regression when the kernel is the NTK [cite47†15 ].\nL176: Despite the fact that NTK analysis often considers the entire model, it is straightforward to specialize it to the fine-tuning case, which we do here for completeness. When using mean squared error, fine-tuning with the linear model is similarly equivalent to solving the kernel regression problem presented in (cite64†14 ). Let $\\mathcal{H}$ be the reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) endowed with a positive definite kernel function $\\mathbf{k}(\\cdot,\\cdot)$, i.e.,\nL177:  | $$\\mathcal{H}=\\bigg\\{f(\\cdot)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_{i}\\mathbf{k}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{x}_{i})\\bigg\\}.$$  |\nL178: \nL179: Assuming the solution lies in or close to this Hilbert space, then as an alternative to (cite51†1 ), we solve\nL180: \nL181:  | $\\displaystyle\\;\\underset{f\\in\\mathcal{H}}{\\textrm{minimize}}\\;\\;\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{\\left(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}\\right)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{T}}\\left\\|f\\left(\\mathbf{x}\\right)-\\mathbf{y}\\right\\|^{2}+\\sigma\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}}^{2},$  |  | (14)\nL182: where $\\|\\cdot\\|_{\\mathcal{H}}$ is the norm corresponding to the inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle_{\\mathcal{H}}$ defined on the RKHS $\\mathcal{H}$. The parameter $\\sigma>0$ regularizes the problem toward minimum-norm solutions and is qualitatively inversely related to the number of gradient descent steps in the original problem.\nL183: We thus effectively formulate the fine-tuning problem as a regularized function estimation in the RKHS, $\\mathcal{H}$, generated by the NTK, $\\mathbf{k}(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x}^{\\prime})=\\nabla f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x})\\nabla f_{\\bm{\\theta}_{0}}(\\mathbf{x}^{\\prime})^{\\top}$. According to the representer theorem  [cite65†19 ], the problem (cite64†14 ) on training dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{T}=\\{\\mathbf{x}_{i},\\mathbf{y}_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n}$ possesses the closed-form solution\nL184:  | $$f^{*}(\\cdot)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\alpha_{i}\\mathbf{k}\\left(\\cdot,\\mathbf{x}_{i}\\right)=\\bm{\\alpha}^{\\top}\\mathbf{K}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{X}),$$  |  | (15)\nL185: \nL186: where $\\mathbf{K}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{X})=[\\mathbf{k}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{x}_{1}),\\ldots,\\mathbf{k}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{x}_{n})]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times n}$ . Substituting (cite66†15 ) in (cite64†14 ), we have\nL187:  | $\\displaystyle\\;\\underset{\\bm{\\alpha}}{\\textrm{minimize}}\\;\\;\\mathbb{E}_{(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{T}}\\left\\|\\bm{\\alpha}^{\\top}\\mathbf{K}(\\cdot,\\mathbf{X})-\\mathbf{Y}\\right\\|^{2}+\\sigma\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}}^{2},$  |  | (16)\nL188: \nL189: which is a convex problem with $\\bm{\\alpha}^{*}=\\left[\\mathbf{K}\\left(\\mathbf{X}_{,}\\mathbf{X}\\right)+\\sigma\\mathbf{I}\\right]^{-1}\\mathbf{Y}$ as the solution. Equivalently,\nL190:  | $\\displaystyle f^{*}(\\cdot)=\\mathbf{K}\\left(\\cdot,\\mathbf{X}\\right)\\left[\\mathbf{K}\\left(\\mathbf{X}_{,}\\mathbf{X}\\right)+\\sigma\\mathbf{I}\\right]^{-1}\\mathbf{Y},$  |  | (17)\nL191: \nL192: where $[\\mathbf{K}(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X})]_{i,j}=\\mathbf{k}(\\mathbf{x}_{i},\\mathbf{x}_{j})$. For the sake of brevity, hereafter, we use $\\mathbf{K}(\\mathbf{X},\\mathbf{X})$ and $\\mathbf{K}$ interchangeably.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Statistical Framework for Alignment with Biased AI Feedback (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08259v1)\nciteturn31649view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view3\",\"lineno\":150}); Total lines: 458\nL127: This objective is biased because $\\widehat{Z}_{i}$ is generated under the AI model $\\widehat{g}$ rather than the ground-truth mechanism $g$. We correct this bias using the human-labeled dataset. Moreover, since human-labeled pairs may be generated under a different response mechanism $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ than the AI-generated data $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{AI}}$, we further account for the resulting distributional shift by incorporating an appropriate density-ratio correction:\nL128:  | $$w(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)}\\mid X):=\\frac{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{AI}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)}\\cdot\\frac{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{AI}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)}.$$  |  | (3.2)\nL129: If $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ is unavailable, we estimate it from data and replace $w$ with an estimator $\\widehat{w}$ constructed from the estimated $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$. We employ the sample splitting technique so that $\\widehat{w}$ is independent of the evaluation sample (cite40†Cai et al., 2024 ; cite41†Cai et al., 2022 ). For notational simplicity, we write $w_{i}$ for either $w(\\cdot)$ or $\\widehat{w}(\\cdot)$ evaluated at $(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})$.\nL130: Thus, the bias induced by replacing $Z$ with $\\widehat{Z}$ can be estimated by the weighted difference:\nL131:  | $$\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}^{B}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}):=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}\\Big\\{\\ell_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},\\widehat{Z}_{i})-\\ell_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},Z_{i})\\Big\\},$$  |  | (3.3)\nL132: \nL133: where $\\widehat{Z}_{i}$ is generated from $(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})$ by the AI evaluator. The debiased loss is then\nL134:  | $$\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}):=\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})-\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}^{B}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}),\\qquad$$  |  | (3.4)\nL135: \nL136: and the estimated policy is defined as $\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}:=\\arg\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})$. The procedure is summarized in Algorithm cite42†1 .\nL137: \nL138: Algorithm 1 Debiased Direct Preference Optimization (DDPO)\nL139: 1: $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{Human}}=\\{(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},Z_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{n}$, $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{AI}}=\\{(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},\\widehat{Z}_{i})\\}_{i=n+1}^{n+N}$, reference policy $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, KL weight $\\beta$.\nL140: \nL141: 2: Compute $w_{i}$ using (cite43†3.2 ) (or $w_{i}=\\widehat{w}_{i}$ via sample splitting).\nL142: \nL143: 3: Form $\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DPO}}^{B}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})$ using (cite44†3.3 ).\nL144: 4: Minimize $\\widehat{L}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}(\\pi;\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})$ in (cite45†3.4 ) by stochastic gradient descent and estimate $\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}$.\nL145: \nL146: 5: return $\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DDPO}}$.\nL147: The proposed debiasing method modifies the DPO objective function to remove the systematic bias introduced by AI evaluators. Our methodology is closely related to the prediction-powered inference (PPI) framework (cite46†Angelopoulos et al., 2023a ; cite47†Angelopoulos et al., 2023b ), which combines labeled data with auxiliary predictions from an external model to improve inference efficiency.\nL148: Unlike PPI, which is primarily designed for inference under the same data-generating distribution, our framework accommodates distributional shifts in the paired responses between AI and human preference data, and we account for this shift through the density-ratio weighting in (cite43†3.2 ).\nL149: ### 3.2 Debiased Identity Preference Optimization\nL150: Although DPO provides a practical and computationally attractive approach to preference alignment, it fundamentally relies on the BT assumption. This assumption can be problematic when preferences are nearly deterministic, as it may lead to overfitting the preference data while ignoring the KL regularization term. To address the limitation, cite30†Azar et al.\nL151: (2024) proposed Identity Preference Optimization (IPO), which seeks the optimal policy $\\pi^{*}=\\arg\\max_{\\pi}\\mathbb{P}(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(\\cdot))$ by maximizing a regularized total preferences\nL152:  | $\\displaystyle\\max_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}$  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(\\cdot))-\\beta D_{\\text{KL}}\\!\\big(\\pi(\\cdot)\\,\\|\\,\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(\\cdot)\\big),$  |  | (3.5)\nL153:  | $\\displaystyle\\text{with}$  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{P}(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(\\cdot)):=\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{X}}\\!\\left[\\mathbb{E}_{Y\\sim\\pi(\\cdot\\mid X),Y^{\\prime}\\sim\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(\\cdot\\mid X)}\\mathbb{P}_{\\text{Human}}(Y\\succ Y^{\\prime}\\mid X)\\right].$  |\nL154: In our setting, we employ an AI evaluator to estimate the preference probability. For each prompt $X$, the AI evaluator outputs either a binary preference label $\\widehat{Z}$ or a pair of evaluation scores $s_{\\mathrm{eva}}(X,Y^{(1)})$ and $s_{\\mathrm{eva}}(X,Y^{(2)})$ for the candidate responses.\nL155: Specifically, for each prompt $X_{i}$, we generate a response $Y_{i}\\sim\\pi(\\cdot\\mid X_{i})$ from the target policy and two responses $Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)}\\sim\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot\\mid X_{i})$ from the reference policy. Then the AI evaluator compares the responses and outputs a preference probability. This procedure yields the following direct estimator:\nL156:  | $$\\widehat{P}_{\\text{DM}}(\\pi):=\\frac{1}{2N}\\sum_{i=n+1}^{n+N}\\{\\tilde{g}(Y_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})+\\tilde{g}(Y_{i},Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})\\}.$$  |  | (3.6)\nL157: In settings where direct access to $\\tilde{g}(Y,Y^{(1)}\\mid X)$ is unavailable, we perform a Monte Carlo approximation. Specifically, for each evaluation, the AI evaluator compares the two responses multiple times and records the preference outcomes for the $j$th comparison as $\\widehat{Z}_{i,j}^{(1)}=\\mathbb{I}(Y_{i}\\succ Y_{i}^{(1)})$ and $\\widehat{Z}_{i,j}^{(2)}=\\mathbb{I}(Y_{i}\\succ Y_{i}^{(2)})$.\nL158: The preference probabilities $\\tilde{g}(X_{i},Y_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)})$ and $\\tilde{g}(X_{i},Y_{i},Y_{i}^{(2)})$ are then approximated by the empirical averages $\\sum_{j=1}^{m}\\widehat{Z}_{i,j}^{(1)}/m$ and $\\sum_{j=1}^{m}\\widehat{Z}_{i,j}^{(2)}/m$, where $m$ denotes the number of comparisons.\nL159: The estimator $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DM}}(\\pi)$ serves as a direct estimator for the unobservable human preference probability. However, it may contain systematic bias when the AI evaluator is misaligned with human preferences. Given the human preference dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{Human}}$, our goal is to construct a debiased estimator of $\\widehat{P}_{DM}(\\pi)$ that accounts for the discrepancy between human and AI preferences.\nL160: Recall that $Z$ and $\\widehat{Z}$ represent the human and AI-labeled preference labels. Here, we characterize the bias as follows:\nL161:  | $$\\begin{split}&\\widehat{P}_{\\text{DM}}(\\pi)-\\mathbb{P}(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(\\cdot))\\approx\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{X}}[\\mathbb{E}_{Y^{(1)}\\sim\\pi(\\cdot\\mid X),Y^{(2)}\\sim\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(\\cdot\\mid X)}(\\widehat{Z}-Z)]\\\\\nL162: &=\\mathbb{E}_{X\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{X}}[\\mathbb{E}_{(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})\\sim\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(\\cdot\\mid X)}(\\widehat{Z}-Z)\\frac{\\pi(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)}\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)}].\\end{split}$$  |\nL163: This expression represents the bias as a weighted expectation of $\\widehat{Z}-Z$, where the weight adjusts for the distributional mismatch between the human- and AI-labeled datasets. Because $Y^{(1)}$ and $Y^{(2)}$ are independently sampled from the generation distribution $\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}$, the bias expression is invariant to the ordering of the response pair. By symmetrizing over $(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)})$, we obtain the following bias estimator based on the dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{Human}}$:\nL164:  | $$\\begin{split}\\widehat{\\mathrm{Bias}}(\\pi):=&\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\\widehat{Z}_{i}-Z_{i})\\frac{\\pi(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}\\\\\nL165: &+\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(Z_{i}-\\widehat{Z}_{i})\\frac{\\pi(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})},\\end{split}$$  |\nL166: where $\\widehat{Z}_{i}$ is generated from $(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})$ by the AI evaluator. We define a debiased estimator of the IPO objective by subtracting the estimated bias,\nL167: \nL168:  | $$\\widehat{P}_{\\text{DIPO}}(\\pi):=\\widehat{P}_{\\text{DM}}(\\pi)-\\widehat{\\text{Bias}}(\\pi).$$  |  | (3.7)\nL169: \nL170: The corresponding debiased policy is obtained by maximizing the corrected objective subject to a KL regularization constraint,\nL171:  | $$\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\text{DIPO}}:=\\arg\\max_{\\pi\\in\\Pi}\\Big\\{\\widehat{P}_{\\text{DIPO}}(\\pi)-\\beta D_{\\text{KL}}\\big(\\pi\\|\\pi_{\\text{ref}}\\big)\\Big\\},$$  |  | (3.8)\nL172: \nL173: By incorporating the bias-corrected preference estimator $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi)$, the resulting policy $\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}$ more closely reflects true human judgments while retaining the stability and regularization properties of IPO. The complete procedure is summarized in Algorithm cite48†2 .\nL174: Algorithm 2 Debiased Identity Preference Optimization (DIPO)\nL175: \nL176: 1: Reference policy $\\pi_{\\text{ref}}$, AI evaluation model, human-labeled dataset $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{Human}}=\\{(X_{i},Y^{(1)}_{i},Y^{(2)}_{i},Z_{i},\\widehat{Z}_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{n}$, KL weight $\\beta$, clipping range $[c_{\\min},c_{\\max}]$, total steps $T$.\nL177: \nL178: 2: Initialize policy $\\pi^{(0)}\\leftarrow\\pi_{\\text{ref}}$.\nL179: \nL180: 3: for $t=1,\\dots,T$ do\nL181: \nL182: 4:   Sample a set of prompts $\\{X_{i}\\}_{i=n+1}^{n+N}$ from the prompt distribution $\\mathcal{D}_{X}$.\nL183: 5:   Sampling: $\\{Y_{i}\\sim\\pi^{(t-1)}(\\cdot\\mid X_{i})\\}_{i=n+1}^{n+N}$ and $\\{(Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)})\\sim\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(\\cdot\\mid X_{i})\\}_{i=n+1}^{n+N}$.\nL184: \nL185: 6:   Compute the direct-model preference estimate, $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DM}}(\\pi^{(t-1)})$, as defined in (cite49†3.6 ).\nL186: 7:   Compute importance ratios with clipping\nL187:  | $$w^{(1)}_{i}=\\text{clip}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{(t-1)}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})},\\,c_{\\min},\\,c_{\\max}\\right),$$  |\nL188:  | $$w^{(2)}_{i}=\\text{clip}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{(t-1)}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}\\frac{\\pi_{\\text{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\text{Gen}}^{\\text{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})},\\,c_{\\min},\\,c_{\\max}\\right).$$  |\nL189:  | $$\\widehat{\\mathrm{Bias}}^{(t-1)}\\;=\\;\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\\widehat{Z}_{i}-Z_{i})\\,w^{(1)}_{i}\\;+\\;\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(Z_{i}-\\widehat{Z}_{i})\\,w^{(2)}_{i}.$$  |\n"
